No. Different-row updates avoid a direct write–write conflict, but not a PostgreSQL SERIALIZABLE read–write dependency cycle. If both transactions count two, Alice reads Bob’s state before Bob changes it, and Bob reads Alice’s state before Alice changes it. Neither could run second in an equivalent serial execution and still count two, so PostgreSQL cannot commit both departures; at least one fails with SQLSTATE 40001.

The referenced original implementation is the standalone SERIALIZABLE transaction below. It has no function entry-point signature or return value: the anonymous DO block conditionally updates Alice, and COMMIT either succeeds or reports an error. This is not the later REPEATABLE READ advisory-lock variant or its READ COMMITTED correction.

```sql
BEGIN TRANSACTION ISOLATION LEVEL SERIALIZABLE;

DO $$
DECLARE
    on_call_count bigint;
BEGIN
    SELECT COUNT(*) INTO on_call_count
    FROM doctors
    WHERE on_call;

    IF on_call_count > 1 THEN
        UPDATE doctors
        SET on_call = FALSE
        WHERE name = 'Alice';
    END IF;
END;
$$;

COMMIT;
```

The original constraints apply: doctors has text primary key name and non-null boolean on_call; initially Alice and Bob are both on call; every writer follows this SERIALIZABLE count-and-conditional-update protocol. Execute the entire script on one PostgreSQL connection; Bob substitutes 'Bob'. No advisory lock is used.

On SQLSTATE 40001, which may occur during a statement or at COMMIT, roll back if necessary and retry everything from BEGIN through COMMIT, including the count and decision, with a fresh snapshot. Retrying only the UPDATE skips the fresh check and could turn off the last doctor.

In any equivalent serial execution, a transaction removes at most one on-call doctor only when the count exceeds one. The first departure leaves one; the next counts one and skips its update. Consequently, committed transactions preserve at least one doctor on call.
